[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Technical Tutorial: Cross-Cluster Search (CCS) and Cross-Cluster Replication (CCR) in Elasticsearch"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"This technical tutorial explores Cross-Cluster Search (CCS) and Cross-Cluster Replication (CCR) in Elasticsearch. These distributed capabilities allow you to scale horizontally across multiple clusters, ensuring high availability, disaster recovery, and unified data exploration without sacrificing query performance."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Prerequisites and Cluster Setup"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before configuring CCS and CCR, ensure that your Elasticsearch clusters can communicate over the transport layer (typically port 9300). You must configure remote cluster connections in your elasticsearch.yml file or dynamically via the cluster update settings API."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"PUT /_cluster/settings
{
  "persistent": {
    "cluster.remote": {
      "cluster_one": {
        "seeds": [
          "192.168.1.101:9300"
        ]
      }
    }
  }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Cross-Cluster Search (CCS)"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Cross-Cluster Search enables any node to act as a federated client, executing search queries across multiple remote clusters simultaneously. This is ideal for centralized logging patterns where indices are grouped by time or region."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Syntax: Reference remote indices using the syntax <cluster_name>:<index_name>."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Network: Uses gateway and transport nodes to efficiently fan out queries and reduce round-trip overhead."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Fault Tolerance: If a remote cluster is unreachable, CCS can fail gracefully depending on the skip_unavailable setting."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Executing a Cross-Cluster Search Query"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"The following example demonstrates how to query a local index alongside a remote index residing on 'cluster_one'."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"GET /local_index,cluster_one:remote_index/_search
{
  "query": {
    "match": {
      "status": "active"
    }
  },
  "ignore_unavailable": true
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Cross-Cluster Replication (CCR)"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Cross-Cluster Replication follows an active-passive model. The cluster containing the original index is designated as the 'leader' cluster, while the cluster receiving the replicated data is the 'follower' cluster. CCR operates at the index level and pulls changes from the leader."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Leader Index: Must have soft deletes enabled (default in modern Elasticsearch versions) to allow the follower to track historical operations."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Follower Index: Created on the local cluster by pointing to the remote leader index and establishing a replication stream."},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Configuring a CCR Follower Index"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"To start replicating an index from a remote leader cluster, use the following API call on your local follower cluster."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"PUT /follower_index/_ccr/follow?wait_for_active_shards=1
{
  "remote_cluster": "cluster_one",
  "leader_index": "leader_index_name"
}"},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Pausing and Resuming Replication"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"During maintenance or network upgrades, you may need to pause replication without losing synchronization state. You can pause the follower stream using the pause API and resume it later."},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"POST /follower_index/_ccr/pause_follow

# To resume replication:
POST /follower_index/_ccr/resume_follow
{
  "remote_cluster": "cluster_one",
  "leader_index": "leader_index_name"
}"},{"order":24,"type":"subheading","text":"Promoting a Follower to a Standalone Index"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the event of a disaster recovery scenario where the leader cluster fails permanently, you can promote the follower index to become a regular, writable standalone index."},{"order":26,"type":"code","text":"POST /follower_index/_ccr/pause_follow
POST /follower_index/_close
POST /follower_index/_ccr/unfollow
POST /follower_index/_open"},{"order":27,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"By combining Cross-Cluster Search and Cross-Cluster Replication, Elasticsearch architects can build highly resilient, globally distributed search topologies. CCS provides low-latency federated querying, while CCR guarantees data redundancy and rapid failover capabilities."}]
